---
import '@fontsource-variable/fraunces';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const sections = [
  {
    h: 'Social and emotional learning works, and the evidence shows it',
    p: [
      'The most influential study on this topic to date comes from Durlak, Weissberg, Dymnicki, Taylor and Schellinger, published in 2011 in the journal Child Development. Their meta-analysis reviewed 213 school-based programmes for social and emotional learning, with data from 270,034 students from kindergarten to high school. Compared with control groups, children in these programmes showed clearly better social and emotional skills, more positive attitudes and better social behaviour. In academic performance, the difference amounted to a gain of eleven percentile points, a remarkable effect for a programme that is really about feelings rather than subject knowledge.',
      'Twelve years later, Cipriano and colleagues updated this research. Their 2023 meta-analysis, also published in Child Development, covers 424 studies from 53 countries with more than 575,000 students. The results confirm the original findings and take them further: programmes for social and emotional learning improve not only skills and academic performance but also the classroom climate, safety at school and relationships between peers.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'What makes a good programme',
    p: [
      'In their analysis, Durlak and colleagues identified four features shared by especially effective programmes, known in the field as the SAFE model: skills are built up step by step (Sequenced), children take part actively instead of just listening (Active), social and emotional skills are the clear focus (Focused), and the learning goals are stated openly (Explicit). Yubuu\'s Feeling Wheel is built on exactly these four principles: the five steps of the game build on one another, children take part rather than listen, and the goal of every round is clear from the start.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'The economic value of early emotional learning',
    p: [
      'In 2015, Belfield and colleagues at the Center for Benefit-Cost Studies in Education at Teachers College, Columbia University, examined the economic value of six well-known SEL programmes in the United States. They counted costs such as materials and staff time, as well as long-term benefits such as lower follow-on costs from less bullying, less substance abuse and better school outcomes. Across all six programmes, the benefit-cost ratio came out at about 1 to 11: every dollar invested created roughly eleven dollars of value for society in the long run.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Why naming a feeling already helps',
    p: [
      'In 2007, neuroscientist Matthew Lieberman and his team at the University of California, Los Angeles, showed in a study published in Psychological Science that simply naming a feeling has measurable effects on the brain. When participants put a feeling into words, activity dropped in the amygdala, the part of the brain that triggers the emotional alarm response, while activity rose in the right ventrolateral prefrontal cortex, a region involved in language and thinking. In research this effect is known as affect labelling. Every round with the Feeling Wheel practises exactly this mechanism, whether or not a solution is found at the end.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Nonviolent Communication as the basis for the needs circles',
    p: [
      'The last step of the game is based on the Nonviolent Communication of psychologist Marshall B. Rosenberg. The basic idea: behind every feeling there is a need, and anyone who understands their own need can express it more clearly, instead of showing it through arguments, withdrawal or blame. This is a skill that can be trained, and that is exactly what the needs circles do at the end of every round.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Learning together',
    p: [
      'According to the sociocultural theory of psychologist Lev Vygotsky, learning doesn\'t happen in isolation but through exchange with other people. Central to this is the zone of proximal development: the space between what a child can already do alone and what they can do with support. In the game, both Yubuu and the other children take on this supporting role. Children listen to one another and, in doing so, deepen their own understanding of feelings.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'Why the material is deliberately physical',
    p: [
      'Research on embodied cognition, shaped among others by Margaret Wilson and Lawrence Barsalou, shows that thinking and learning are closely tied to physical experience. People often understand and remember things better when they experience them with their bodies instead of only hearing or reading about them. That is why Yubuu\'s Feeling Wheel is deliberately designed as a hands-on material: wood to touch, magnets to place yourself, cards to discover, with no worksheet and no screen.',
    ],
  },
  {
    h: 'An honest note on the current state of research',
    p: [
      'The studies above show that social and emotional learning works, and they support the individual principles that Yubuu\'s Feeling Wheel is built on. There is not yet a study of Yubuu\'s Feeling Wheel itself. One is planned as part of the pilot projects from 2027. This openness matters to us: we build on solid research, but we don\'t promise results that haven\'t yet been studied specifically.',
    ],
  },
];

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

const sources = [
  { ref: 'Belfield, C., Bowden, B., Klapp, A., Levin, H., Shand, R., Zander, S. (2015). The Economic Value of Social and Emotional Learning. Center for Benefit-Cost Studies in Education, Teachers College, Columbia University.', href: 'https://wymancenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/11/Belfield-et-al-2015.pdf' },
  { ref: 'Cipriano, C., Strambler, M. J., Naples, L. H., Ha, C., Kirk, M., Wood, M. et al. (2023). The State of Evidence for Social and Emotional Learning: A Contemporary Meta-Analysis of Universal School-Based SEL Interventions. Child Development, 94, 1181–1204.', href: 'https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/cdev.13968', extra: { label: 'Free preprint', href: 'https://osf.io/wscgq/' } },
  { ref: 'Durlak, J. A., Weissberg, R. P., Dymnicki, A. B., Taylor, R. D., Schellinger, K. B. (2011). The Impact of Enhancing Students’ Social and Emotional Learning: A Meta-Analysis of School-Based Universal Interventions. Child Development, 82(1), 405–432.', href: 'https://srcd.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/j.1467-8624.2010.01564.x', extra: { label: 'Free PDF', href: 'https://casel.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/meta-analysis-child-development-1.pdf' } },
  { ref: 'Lieberman, M. D., Eisenberger, N. I., Crockett, M. J., Tom, S. M., Pfeifer, J. H., Way, B. M. (2007). Putting Feelings Into Words: Affect Labeling Disrupts Amygdala Activity in Response to Affective Stimuli. Psychological Science, 18(5), 421–428.', href: 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17576282/', extra: { label: 'Free PDF', href: 'https://sanlab.psych.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/31/2015/05/Lieberman_AL-2007.pdf' } },
  { ref: 'Rosenberg, M. B. (2003). Nonviolent Communication: A Language of Life.', href: 'https://www.cnvc.org', label: 'About the concept (Center for Nonviolent Communication)' },
  { ref: 'Vygotsky, L. S. (1978). Mind in Society: The Development of Higher Psychological Processes.', href: 'https://archive.org/details/mindinsocietydev0000vygo', label: 'Free full text (Archive.org)' },
];
---
<Base lang="en" title="The Science Behind It — EMORIA" current=""
  description="The research behind Yubuu's Feeling Wheel: social and emotional learning, affect labelling, Nonviolent Communication and embodied cognition, explained in plain language.">

  <article class="relative mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-28 print:py-8">
    <img src={`${base}img/yubuu-telescope.png`} alt="Yubuu with a telescope on star charts" loading="lazy"
      class="pointer-events-none absolute left-full top-16 ml-8 hidden h-52 w-auto drop-shadow-[0_18px_28px_rgba(43,42,40,0.16)] xl:block print:hidden" />
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-[var(--color-terracotta)]">Background</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 font-serif-heading text-3xl sm:text-4xl">The Science Behind It</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">Yubuu's Feeling Wheel is not a random collection of ideas. It follows several well-established approaches from education, developmental psychology, neuroscience and communication theory. Here we explain which research stands behind each building block.</p>

    <div class="mt-12 space-y-10">
      {sections.map((s) => (
        <section>
          <h2 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-xl font-semibold">{s.h}</h2>
          {s.p.map((para) => <p class="mt-3 text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">{para}</p>)}
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <section class="mt-16 border-t border-[var(--color-cream-edge)] pt-10">
      <h2 class="font-[var(--font-display)] text-xl font-semibold">Sources</h2>
      <ol class="mt-4 space-y-5">
        {sources.map((s) => (
          <li class="text-sm text-[var(--color-ink-soft)]">
            {s.ref}
            <br />
            <a href={s.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] underline decoration-dotted underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-terracotta)]">
              {s.label ?? 'Zur Studie'}
            </a>
            {s.extra && (
              <>
                {' · '}
                <a href={s.extra.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-[var(--color-ink-faint)] underline decoration-dotted underline-offset-2 hover:text-[var(--color-terracotta)]">
                  {s.extra.label}
                </a>
              </>
            )}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>
